How the real A32 machine prepares its first QR call
==================================================

This is a deliberately bottom-up symbolic account of the actual
instruction bytes 0x0000 through 0x005c in the original
../dqrls.armv7-a32.disasm.txt. No mathematical QR operation is
assumed as an explanation. The x86 counterpart is separately
preserved in English Major C draft PR #7 at
_/captures/r-lm-native-x86-64/english-translation/
Following the first actual QR call one memory operation at a time.english

This account is a manually derived machine-state correspondence.
It is NOT an observed ARM runtime trace, formal ARM instruction
verification, or QEMU execution of the softfp object. It uses the
AAPCS32 base procedure-call contract of the historical softfp build.

Begin with exactly named pointers
---------------------------------
Let EntryStackPointer be the 32-bit byte address in SP when the
dqrls_ routine first begins executing. AAPCS32 provides the first
four pointer arguments in R0, R1, R2 and R3, and the additional
pointer arguments in consecutive four-byte stack slots beginning
at EntryStackPointer. The routine signature is:

  dqrls(x, n, p, y, ny, tol, b, rsd, qty, k, jpvt, qraux, work)

The incoming register values before instruction 0x0000 are:
  R0 = PointerToXArray.
  R1 = PointerToNInteger.
  R2 = PointerToPInteger.
  R3 = PointerToYArray.

The original stack contents include:
  [EntryStackPointer +  0] = PointerToNYInteger;
  [EntryStackPointer +  4] = PointerToToleranceDouble;
  [EntryStackPointer +  8] = PointerToBArray;
  [EntryStackPointer + 12] = PointerToResidualArray;
  [EntryStackPointer + 16] = PointerToQtyArray;
  [EntryStackPointer + 20] = PointerToKInteger;
  [EntryStackPointer + 24] = PointerToPivotArray;
  [EntryStackPointer + 28] = PointerToQRAuxiliaryArray;
  [EntryStackPointer + 32] = PointerToWorkArray.

These are POINTERS, because this Fortran interface passes arguments
by reference. An integer dimension pointer is not itself a dimension.
Do not apply this entry layout to the x86-64 calling convention:
that other architecture has six initial pointer argument registers
and a different machine call stack.

Instruction-by-instruction preparation
-------------------------------------
0x0000, machine word E92D4FF0, PUSH {R4,R5,R6,R7,R8,R9,SL,FP,LR}:
  Save nine old 32-bit register values, allocating 36 bytes in
  the full-descending stack. SP now equals EntryStackPointer - 36.
  SL means R10 and FP means R11; they are not extra registers.

0x0004, E1A06003, MOV R6,R3:
  Preserve the original Y-array pointer in R6. No array data is
  dereferenced. R3 still contains PointerToYArray.

0x0008, E592C000, LDR IP,[R2]:
  Read four bytes from PointerToPInteger and store their raw bits
  in IP (R12). This is the actual integer p, not its address.

0x000C, E24DD04C, SUB SP,SP,#76:
  Allocate another 76 bytes below the nine saved registers.
  SP now equals EntryStackPointer - 112. Call this current
  pointer FrameStackPointer. No P or Y array element is changed.

0x0010, E5914000, LDR R4,[R1]:
  Read the four bytes of integer n into R4.

0x0014, E1A03002, MOV R3,R2:
  Replace R3 with PointerToPInteger. This overwrites its earlier
  Y pointer copy but Y was saved in R6 at 0x0004.

0x0018, E1A0A001, MOV SL,R1:
  Save PointerToNInteger in SL (R10).

0x001C, E1CC8FCC, BIC R8,IP,IP,ASR #31:
  Shift the sign bit of IP across a 32-bit mask. If p was negative,
  the mask is all ones and R8 becomes zero; otherwise the mask
  is zero and R8 keeps the 32-bit p bit pattern. This is a
  machine-selected nonnegative bound, not the original p pointer.

0x0020, E59DC074, LDR IP,[SP,#116]:
  FrameStackPointer + 116 equals EntryStackPointer + 4.
  Read PointerToToleranceDouble into IP. This reads one pointer,
  not the floating-point tolerance itself.

0x0024, E1C44FC4, BIC R4,R4,R4,ASR #31:
  Similarly convert negative signed n into zero, or retain its
  original nonnegative 32-bit representation. Do not call this
  an unsigned absolute-value computation.

0x0028, E59D2084, LDR R2,[SP,#132]:
  FrameStackPointer + 132 = EntryStackPointer + 20.
  Read PointerToKInteger into R2.

0x002C, E58DC000, STR IP,[SP]:
  Store the tolerance pointer at FrameStackPointer + 0, the
  location of the fifth argument in the next AAPCS32 call.

0x0030, E59DC088, LDR IP,[SP,#136]:
  Read PointerToPivotArray from EntryStackPointer + 24.

0x0034, E58D2004, STR R2,[SP,#4]:
  Store PointerToKInteger into the next call's sixth-argument
  slot at FrameStackPointer + 4.

0x0038, E58DC00C, STR IP,[SP,#12]:
  Store PointerToPivotArray into the next call's eighth-argument
  slot at FrameStackPointer + 12.

0x003C, E59D208C, LDR R2,[SP,#140]:
  Read PointerToQRAuxiliaryArray from EntryStackPointer + 28.

0x0040, E59DC090, LDR IP,[SP,#144]:
  Read PointerToWorkArray from EntryStackPointer + 32.

0x0044, E58D2008, STR R2,[SP,#8]:
  Store PointerToQRAuxiliaryArray in the seventh-argument slot.

0x0048, E1A02001, MOV R2,R1:
  Replace R2 (which had contained qraux) with PointerToNInteger,
  so the next call's THIRD argument is another pointer to n.

0x004C, E58DC010, STR IP,[SP,#16]:
  Store PointerToWorkArray into the ninth-argument slot.

0x0050, E58D0028, STR R0,[SP,#40]:
  Save PointerToXArray in private local stack storage for later
  use when this routine resumes.

0x0054, E58D302C, STR R3,[SP,#44]:
  Save PointerToPInteger in private local storage.

0x0058, E58D8038, STR R8,[SP,#56]:
  Save the calculated nonnegative p bound in private local storage.

0x005C, EBFFFFFE, BL 0 <dqrdc2_>:
  Store the address of the following A32 instruction, 0x0060
  in this unlinked coordinate system, in LR, and transfer control
  to the symbol dqrdc2_ after the R_ARM_CALL linker relocation
  has been resolved. The printed object-file destination 0 is a
  relocation placeholder, NOT the literal runtime call target.

The exact outbound call arguments at 0x005C are therefore:
  R0 => x (PointerToXArray).
  R1 => n (PointerToNInteger).
  R2 => n (PointerToNInteger again).
  R3 => p (PointerToPInteger).
  [FrameStackPointer +  0] => tol.
  [FrameStackPointer +  4] => k.
  [FrameStackPointer +  8] => qraux.
  [FrameStackPointer + 12] => jpvt.
  [FrameStackPointer + 16] => work.

Now, and only after deriving those pointers, cross-check the
source Fortran statement in ../../r-lm-native-x86-64/dqrls.f,
which reads:

  call dqrdc2(x,n,n,p,tol,k,qraux,jpvt,work)

Every argument matches the independently inspected ARM machine
preparation. This establishes a concrete source/calling-boundary
correspondence for these entry instructions and under the stated
AAPCS32 premises. It does not establish the effect of dqrdc2_'s
unexamined arithmetic on its input arrays, or of BLAS library calls.

Why this is different from the x86-64 entry machine
---------------------------------------------------
The corresponding x86-64 object moves six arguments through
RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8 and R9 before pushing additional pointers
onto its stack, and its CALL pushes a return address there.
This ARM routine receives only four pointer arguments in R0-R3,
uses a preallocated stack frame for five outbound arguments, and
its BL stores the return address in LR. Both are legitimate
machine-specific ways to enter the same R Fortran routine.
Neither is the other's bytewise instruction translation.

In both cases the true next execution index must account for
the architecture's instruction width and branching rules:
A32 ordinary completion advances the byte address by four,
while x86-64 uses the actual variable instruction length.
An ARM-state read of PC as a DATA operand is generally A+8,
not the A+4 next-instruction address.

Evidence and unresolved boundaries
----------------------------------
This derivation is exact about the listed pointer and stack
arithmetic under the declared entry ABI; it is not a trace
from executing the object under QEMU. To validate a complete
numerical result, the linked runtime must also specify dynamic
BLAS, Fortran runtime, memory mappings, VFP status, and calls.
The separate parent ARM hardfp linked numerical smoke is
different from this softfp object and must never be used as
evidence of ABI identity or an exact instruction trace for this
unlinked specimen.
